@node Agent Desktops
@chapter Agent desktops: a private screen for each brigade agent

An agent that drives GUI programs on the screen you are sitting at
fights you for the pointer and the focus.  An agent desktop gives it a
screen nobody is sitting at: a private, headless gowl with its own
Wayland socket, seat, focus and clipboard.  The agent's programs open
there, the agent looks at it and drives it through gowl's own MCP
server, and you can watch it and take it over.

It is clawtilla's ``display computer''
(@file{deps/clawtilla/docs/computers.org}, the @code{gowl} backend)
restated in Elisp, so a brigade agent needs no clawtilla daemon.  It is
@emph{not} isolation: the programs run as you, on your files.  The
screen is the safe part.  Combine it with @code{worktree} or
@code{podman} when you want the rest.

The code is @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-brigade-desktop.el} (lifecycle, the
JSON-RPC client, the relay, the isolation providers, the agent's tools,
the dashboard panel) and @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-brigade-desktop-view.el}
(the viewer).  There is no C.  Everything is processes, sentinels,
timers and network processes with callbacks, because cmacs may itself
be the compositor of the session (@command{emacs --gowl}), where a
blocked Emacs is a frozen desktop.

