@node Agent Desktop Quick Start
@section Quick start

@lisp
;; An agent with a desktop.
(cmacs-brigade-register-agent
 :name 'painter
 :prompt "You drive GUI programs on your own desktop."
 :isolation 'desktop
 :tools '(desktop))

;; Or by hand.
(cmacs-brigade-desktop-start "scratch")
(cmacs-brigade-desktop-view "scratch")   ; t takes control, l launches
(cmacs-brigade-desktop-stop "scratch")
@end lisp

@node Agent Desktop Requirements
@section Requirements

A gowl built with its MCP module (@command{make MCP=1} in
@file{deps/gowl}; Fedora build packages @code{libsoup3-devel},
@code{libdex-devel}, @code{libpng-devel}).  The MCP module is where
pictures and input come from, so a gowl without it cannot be looked at
or driven and is passed over.  @code{desktop_text} and gowl's
@code{screen_text} need @code{tesseract} and
@code{tesseract-langpack-eng}.  @command{setpriv} (util-linux) is used
when present.

@strong{The in-tree gowl that cmacs builds has no MCP module}, so on a
development tree the search skips it and uses the installed one, and
says so in the status:

@example
deps/gowl/build/release/gowl has no mcp module (built without MCP=1)
@end example

@node Agent Desktop Lifecycle
@section Lifecycle

@table @code
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-start @var{id} &optional @var{callback}
Start a desktop keyed by an agent or task id.  Returns at once;
@var{callback} is called with @var{id} and non-@code{nil} when it is
ready, or @var{id} and @code{nil} when it could not be started.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-stop @var{id}
Stop it and every program on it.  Safe to call twice.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-list
The ids there are.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-status @var{id}
A plist: @code{:state} (@code{locating}, @code{starting}, @code{ready},
@code{failed}, @code{stopped}), @code{:reason}, @code{:notes},
@code{:held}, @code{:program}, @code{:pid}, @code{:width},
@code{:height}, @code{:layout-width}, @code{:layout-height},
@code{:scale}, @code{:wayland}, @code{:relay}, @code{:directory},
@code{:log}.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-take @var{id}
@itemx cmacs-brigade-desktop-release @var{id}
@itemx cmacs-brigade-desktop-held-p @var{id}
The takeover.
@end table

@subheading Finding a gowl

With @code{cmacs-brigade-desktop-gowl-program} @code{nil} the candidates
are, in order: @file{deps/gowl/build/release/gowl} and
@file{deps/gowl/build/debug/gowl} under
@code{cmacs-brigade-desktop-source-directory} (default
@code{source-directory}), then every @command{gowl} on @code{exec-path},
then @file{/usr/bin} and @file{/usr/sbin}.  Each is asked two things,
asynchronously, with a five second limit each:

@enumerate
@item
@option{--version} must exit 0 and say something.  A gowl that cannot
load its wlroots fails here and nowhere earlier.
@item
@option{--list-modules-ex} must list an @code{mcp} module.  A gowl too
old to know the flag is given the benefit of the doubt.
@end enumerate

What was skipped is kept in @code{:notes}.  A binary that answered is
remembered with its modification time and not asked again until it
changes.  A pinned path is used instead of searching and must still
answer @option{--version}.

The standalone gowl finds its modules next to its own binary and then
in its compiled-in module directory; it reads no environment variable
for that (@env{CMACS_GOWL_MODULE_DIR} is read by cmacs's embedded gowl
only), so nothing is exported.

@subheading How gowl is started

@command{gowl --config DIR/gowl.yaml --no-c-config}, under
@command{setpriv --pdeathsig SIGTERM} when @command{setpriv} exists
(@code{cmacs-brigade-desktop-use-pdeathsig}), with @code{make-process}
and an argument list, never a shell string.  Its working directory is
your home, because that is what its @code{spawn} hands to programs.  Its
environment is yours plus:

@example
XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=DIR/run            its sockets, not yours
WLR_BACKENDS=headless              named outright: left to choose, wlroots
WLR_HEADLESS_OUTPUTS=1             would find WAYLAND_DISPLAY and open a
WLR_RENDERER=pixman                window on your screen
WLR_LIBINPUT_NO_DEVICES=1
GOWL_OUTPUT_SIZE=1280x800          cmacs-brigade-desktop-resolution
GOWL_DISABLE_SYSTEMD=1             never touches your session's units
@end example

@noindent
and with @env{WAYLAND_DISPLAY}, @env{WAYLAND_SOCKET}, @env{DISPLAY},
@env{GOWL_SOCKET} and @env{GOWL_MCP_SOCKET} removed.

The generated @file{gowl.yaml} is exactly this, and none of your gowl
configuration is read:

@example
# Written by cmacs for an agent's headless desktop; rewritten at start.
modules:
  ipc:
    enabled: true
  mcp:
    enabled: true
    transport-stdio: true
    transport-http: false
@end example

@subheading Ready, failed, stopped

A timer looks every tenth of a second.  The desktop is @code{ready} when
@file{gowl.sock}, @file{gowl-mcp.sock} and @file{wayland-0} exist in
@file{run/}.  It is @code{failed}, with gowl's last lines as the reason,
when gowl exits first or @code{cmacs-brigade-desktop-start-timeout}
(15 s) passes.  Nothing waits: callers hand over a callback.

The Wayland socket's name is known before gowl starts.  @file{run/} is
emptied at each start and holds only that gowl's files, so the first
free name is always @file{wayland-0}; that is what lets the environment
be handed to an agent while gowl is still coming up.  A gowl that
opened any other name is stopped and the start failed, since its
programs were told @file{wayland-0}.

Stopping sends SIGTERM to the process that was started, through its
process object, and SIGKILL three seconds later if it is still there.
Never a pattern: this machine runs other gowls, the session's among
them.  Every desktop is stopped from @code{kill-emacs-hook};
@command{setpriv --pdeathsig} covers a crash.

@subheading Files

@code{cmacs-brigade-desktop-directory}, by default
@file{$XDG_STATE_HOME/cmacs/brigade/desktops/}, one 0700 directory per
desktop:

@table @file
@item gowl.yaml
The generated config (0600).
@item server.log
gowl's output (0600).  A log that is a symlink or not a plain file is
refused at start.
@item run/
gowl's @env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}: @file{wayland-0}, @file{gowl.sock},
@file{gowl-mcp.sock}.  Removed when the desktop stops.
@item relay.sock
The filtered relay (0600).  Removed when the desktop stops.
@item shots/screen.png
The last picture taken for an agent (0600).  Removed when the desktop
stops.
@end table

An id that is a plain file name is used as the directory name; anything
else (a slash, a space, a leading dot) becomes a hash, because an id
reaches here from a plan file and must not be able to name a path.

A unix socket address holds 107 bytes.  A directory deep enough that
@file{run/gowl-mcp.sock} would not fit is refused at start, before
anything is made, with the path named.

@node Agent Desktop Options
@section Options

@table @code
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-gowl-program
@code{nil} (search).  Pin a gowl.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-source-directory
@code{nil} (@code{source-directory}).  Where the in-tree build is looked
for.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-relay-program
@code{nil} (search).  The @command{gowl-mcp} named in an agent's MCP
config.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-resolution
@code{"1280x800"}.  Each side 64 to 8192.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-scale
@code{1.0}.  Applied over IPC @code{monitor.configure} once up; the
layout is then the resolution divided by the scale.  Leave it at 1
unless the programs are unreadable: a model clicking on a screenshot
expects the picture's pixels to be the coordinates.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-directory
@file{$XDG_STATE_HOME/cmacs/brigade/desktops}.  Keep it short.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-start-timeout
15 seconds.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-call-timeout
30 seconds per call.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-use-pdeathsig
@code{t}.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-grants
@code{(look act spawn)}.  The default grants.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-agent-grants
@code{nil}.  @code{((AGENT . GRANTS) ...)}.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-podman-args
@code{nil}.  Extra @command{podman run} arguments for
@code{desktop-podman}.
@item cmacs-brigade-desktop-view-interval
1.0 seconds between pictures in the viewer.
@end table

@node Agent Desktop Isolation
@section Isolation providers

Registered through the public @code{cmacs-brigade-register-isolation},
like the shipped three.

@table @code
@item desktop
@code{none} with a screen.
@item desktop-worktree
A desktop and the @code{worktree} provider's git worktree (its cwd,
both environments).
@item desktop-podman
A desktop and the @code{podman} provider's container, with the Wayland
socket file mounted.
@end table

The registry has no way to combine two providers, so the combinations
are kinds of their own.  They call the shipped providers' own prepare
and teardown functions; if the second half fails, the first is torn
down.

@subheading What prepare returns

@lisp
(:cwd "/where/it/was/"
 :env (("WAYLAND_DISPLAY" . "/.../desktops/task-1/run/wayland-0")
       ("GDK_BACKEND" . "wayland")
       ("QT_QPA_PLATFORM" . "wayland")
       ("DISPLAY" . "")
       ("CMACS_BRIGADE_DESKTOP" . "task-1"))
 :desktop "task-1"
 :mcp-servers ((:name "desktop"
                :command "/usr/bin/gowl-mcp"
                :args ("--socket" "/.../desktops/task-1/relay.sock"))))
@end lisp

@code{:cwd} and @code{:env} are the existing contract and reach every
worker today, so a CLI agent's own shell opens programs on its desktop.
@env{WAYLAND_DISPLAY} is the socket's absolute path, which libwayland
takes as it is and which survives a sandbox replacing
@env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}.  @env{DISPLAY} is emptied rather than unset
because a worker's environment is a list of @samp{NAME=VALUE} and cannot
unset; the nested gowl's XWayland display is known only to gowl, and a
program started through gowl's own spawn (@code{desktop_launch})
inherits it.

@code{:desktop} and @code{:mcp-servers} are @emph{additions} to the
contract.  @code{:mcp-servers} is the MCP endpoint for gowl's own tools:
the relay, never gowl's socket.  A run hands it to
@code{cmacs-brigade-host-provision}, which writes each server into the
CLI agent's MCP config beside @code{cmacs}; see
@ref{Agent Desktop CLI Agents}.

Prepare returns while gowl is still starting.  The agent's desktop
tools wait for the desktop themselves.

@subheading desktop-podman

The container is given the Wayland socket @emph{file}, at the same
path, and the three variables.  Never the directory: @file{gowl.sock}
and @file{gowl-mcp.sock} sit beside it and both answer @code{spawn} by
running a command on the host.

This is the one place that waits.  A container's mounts are fixed when
it is created, and the mount is of a socket that does not exist until
gowl has started.  The wait is bounded by the start timeout and spent
in @code{accept-process-output}; the @command{podman run} right after
it blocks for longer.

The mount is made through a new variable in
@file{cmacs-brigade-isolation.el},
@code{cmacs-brigade-isolation-podman-extra-args}, bound around the
shipped prepare function.

With SELinux enforcing, a container is normally denied a connection to
a socket owned by an unconfined process.
@code{cmacs-brigade-desktop-podman-args} is where you decide what to do
about that (@code{("--security-opt" "label=disable")} is the usual
way).  It is empty by default because that is a decision, not a
default.  @strong{Not verified on a real container.}

@node Agent Desktop Grants
@section What the agent may do

Three grants, clawtilla's split:

@table @code
@item look
gowl's @code{list_clients}, @code{list_monitors}, @code{get_tag_state},
@code{get_config}, @code{list_keybinds}, @code{list_modules},
@code{get_focused_client}, @code{describe_desktop}, @code{find_window},
@code{get_client_process_info}, @code{screenshot_client},
@code{screenshot_monitor}, @code{screenshot_region}, @code{screen_text},
@code{pick_color}; the brigade's @code{desktop_screenshot},
@code{desktop_windows}, @code{desktop_text}.
@item act
gowl's @code{focus_client}, @code{close_client},
@code{move_client_to_tag}, @code{set_client_state}, @code{resize_client},
@code{move_client}, @code{swap_clients}, @code{zoom_client},
@code{view_tag}, @code{toggle_tag_view}, @code{set_client_tags},
@code{set_mfact}, @code{set_nmaster}, @code{send_key}, @code{send_text},
@code{send_mouse}, @code{send_mouse_move}, @code{send_scroll}; the
brigade's @code{desktop_key}, @code{desktop_type}, @code{desktop_click},
@code{desktop_scroll}.
@item spawn
gowl's @code{spawn}; the brigade's @code{desktop_launch}.
@item @r{never}
@code{start_recording}, @code{drain_recording}, @code{stop_recording},
@code{recording_status}, @code{macro_record}.
@end table

All three are on by default because the screen is private.  Recording
is granted by nothing, whatever is written in the option.  A tool in no
class is not relayed: gowl's server also has @code{compositor_quit},
@code{signal_client}, @code{add_keybind}, the clipboard history and the
macro, broadcast and input-remap tools, and a tool gowl gains later has
to be put in a class by somebody before an agent can call it.

@lisp
(setq cmacs-brigade-desktop-agent-grants '((reviewer look)))
@end lisp

@node Agent Desktop Tools
@section The agent's tools

Defined with @code{cmacs-brigade-deftool}, group @code{desktop}, all
asynchronous.  The acting ones are marked destructive, so a read-only
agent only looks.

@table @code
@item desktop_screenshot
A picture; returns the PNG's path and size.  Needs @code{look}.
@item desktop_windows
The windows open, as JSON.  Needs @code{look}.
@item desktop_text
The text on screen, or in @code{x}, @code{y}, @code{width},
@code{height} (OCR).  Needs @code{look}.
@item desktop_launch
Start @code{command}; returns at once.  Needs @code{spawn}.
@item desktop_key
One key (@code{Return}) or a combination (@code{Ctrl+s}).  Needs
@code{act}.
@item desktop_type
Type @code{text} into the focused window.  Needs @code{act}.
@item desktop_click
Click @code{button} at @code{x}, @code{y}.  Needs @code{act}.
@item desktop_scroll
Scroll @code{direction} by @code{amount} at @code{x}, @code{y}.  Needs
@code{act}.
@end table

@strong{None of them takes a desktop.}  The desktop is the calling
task's, and the task is what the brigade says it is, not what the model
writes.  An agent that could name a desktop could name another agent's;
one that could name a socket could name the session's.  A call from no
task (a chat buffer, an external MCP client) and a task with no desktop
are both refused in a sentence.

A tool's handler is not told who is calling.
@code{cmacs-brigade-before-tool-call-functions} is, and runs
immediately before the handler, so the caller is noted there and taken
by the handler as its first act.

Tool results are text, so @code{desktop_screenshot} writes the picture
to @file{shots/screen.png} in the desktop's own directory (one file,
replaced each time, removed with the desktop) and returns its path.  An
agent whose MCP client shows images gets real ones from gowl's own
@code{screenshot_monitor} through the relay.

How each reaches gowl:

@itemize @bullet
@item
A click is @code{send_mouse_move} then @code{send_mouse}; a scroll is a
move then @code{send_scroll}.  gowl's tools act where the pointer
already is.
@item
One key is the MCP @code{send_key}, which takes exactly one XKB key
name.  A combination goes to the IPC socket's @code{window.send_key},
the one that understands modifiers.
@item
@code{desktop_launch} is the IPC socket's @code{spawn} (it returns the
pid), falling back to the MCP @code{spawn} tool.
@end itemize

@node Agent Desktop Relay
@section The relay

gowl's MCP socket answers anybody who can reach it, with every tool.
An agent is never given it.  It is given @file{relay.sock}, a server
inside cmacs that passes lines to gowl and back and looks at three
things on the way:

@table @asis
@item @code{tools/list}
The reply is cut down to the tools the agent's grants admit; a tool it
may not use is not offered at all.
@item @code{tools/call}
A tool outside its grants, or an acting tool while a person holds the
desktop, is answered by the relay with an @code{isError} result and
never reaches gowl.
@item @code{initialize}, @code{ping}, @code{notifications/*}, replies
Passed through.
@item a batch (a JSON array)
Refused whole: a call inside an array is still a call.
@item any other method
@code{-32601}.
@end table

Bytes from gowl go straight through unless a tool list is owed, so a
screenshot (megabytes on one line) is not copied line by line.

@command{gowl-mcp --socket .../relay.sock} is what goes in an agent's
MCP config; @command{gowl-mcp} is gowl's own stdio-to-socket bridge and
does no filtering of its own.

@node Agent Desktop Viewer
@section Watching, and taking over

@kbd{M-x cmacs-brigade-desktop-view} shows the desktop as a picture,
taken again every @code{cmacs-brigade-desktop-view-interval} seconds
@emph{while the buffer is on screen}.  A buffer nobody is looking at
asks for nothing.  The picture never touches the disk: the image is
made from the bytes gowl sent.  It is scaled to fit the window, and a
click is mapped back to the desktop's own coordinates.

@table @kbd
@item t
Take control.
@item r
@itemx C-c C-r
Release (only @kbd{C-c C-r} while you hold it).
@item C-c C-k
Send a key or combination by name (@code{Ctrl+s}, @code{Alt+F4}).
@item l
@itemx C-c C-l
Launch a program (you need not hold the desktop).
@item g
Take a picture now.
@item q
Bury the view, releasing the desktop if you held it.
@end table

While you hold it the agent's acting tools, the brigade's and gowl's,
are refused with:

@example
A person has taken control of this desktop, so desktop_click was not done.
Looking still works -- screenshots, window lists, reading text -- so wait and
try again once they release it.
@end example

Looking is not refused, so the agent can wait usefully.  And while you
hold it, what you type and the clicks and scrolling you do on the
picture go to the desktop: printing keys as text, and Return, Tab,
BackSpace, Escape, Delete, the arrows, Home, End, Page Up and Down and
F1 to F12 as keys.  Emacs keeps @kbd{C-x}, @kbd{M-x} and its other
prefixes; that is why releasing is @kbd{C-c C-r} and not @kbd{r}, and
why a combination is sent by name.

Killing the view buffer releases the desktop too.  A desktop held by a
buffer nobody can see is an agent stopped for no reason anybody
remembers.

The brigade dashboard has a @emph{Desktops} panel: id, state, size,
whether you hold it, and why it failed.

@node Agent Desktop Security
@section Security

@itemize @bullet
@item
The display is reachable by anything running as you that can reach its
socket, in a 0700 directory.  It is not a boundary against your own
processes.
@item
gowl runs with only its IPC and MCP modules, its MCP on a unix socket
only, and none of your configuration.  Its runtime directory is its
own, so it cannot replace your session's @file{gowl.sock}.
@item
An agent gets the relay, never @file{gowl.sock} or
@file{gowl-mcp.sock}.  A sandbox gets the Wayland socket file, never
@file{run/}.
@item
The state directory is not under any agent's working directory.
@end itemize

@node Agent Desktop CLI Agents
@section CLI agents

A CLI agent (@code{claude-code}, @code{opencode}, @code{codex},
@code{grok-build}) is another process, so it reaches its desktop over
MCP.  Both halves are wired.

@subheading The desktop's server is in its config

@code{cmacs-brigade--start-now} passes the prepared plist's
@code{:mcp-servers} to @code{cmacs-brigade-host-provision} as
@code{:extra-servers}, and each config dialect's emitter writes them
beside the @code{cmacs} server:

@multitable @columnfractions .15 .2 .65
@headitem Dialect @tab Agent @tab Where the @code{desktop} server lands
@item @code{claude} @tab claude-code
@tab @code{mcpServers.desktop}: @code{command}, @code{args}, @code{env}
@item @code{opencode} @tab opencode
@tab @code{mcp.desktop}: @code{type: local}, one @code{command} array,
@code{environment}
@item @code{grok} @tab grok-build
@tab @code{[mcp_servers.cmacs-brigade-desktop]}
@item @code{codex} @tab codex
@tab the same table, in the overlay @file{CODEX_HOME/config.toml}
@end multitable

The TOML dialects prefix the name with @code{cmacs-brigade-}, for the
reason the @code{cmacs} server is called @code{cmacs-brigade} there:
grok merges config sources by server name, and a name a project's own
config also uses makes the entry repo-local and silently unstarted.  In
those agents the tools are therefore the @code{cmacs-brigade-desktop}
server's.

The servers are passed to the emitter exactly as the provider returned
them.  Every conversion a dialect needs (an @code{:env} plist or alist,
a TOML string) happens inside that dialect's emitter, and
@code{cmacs-brigade-host--extra-servers} refuses a server no dialect
could write faithfully: no @code{:command}, a @code{:name} that is not
@samp{[A-Za-z0-9_-]+}, a duplicate, or one of the brigade's own names
(@code{cmacs}, @code{cmacs-brigade}), which would replace the scoped
server.  A refusal fails the start with a reason; it does not start an
agent that is missing the server its isolation promised.

@subheading A desktop call knows its task

The agent's @code{cmacs} server is a @emph{scoped socket}
(@code{cmacs-brigade-scope-open}).  The run opens it with the task id as
a second argument, the scope record in the editor keeps it, and every
session accepted on that socket carries it.  The MCP handler reads it
from there and evaluates
@code{(cmacs-brigade-call-tool NAME ARGS "mcp" nil TASK)}, so the
before-tool-call hook sees @code{:task} exactly as it does for an
in-process agent, and @code{desktop_*} act on that task's desktop.

@itemize
@item
The id never comes from the client.  It is not in the config file, and
no argument is read for it: a @code{task}, @code{desktop} or @code{id}
member in a call's arguments is ignored, as it is in-process.
@item
A scope opened for task A cannot reach task B's desktop.  There is
nothing it could send that names one.
@item
The plain @file{cmacs-mcp-<pid>.sock}, and a scope opened for no task (a
harness or chat buffer's), carry no task.  @code{desktop_*} answer
@samp{this call came from no brigade task}.
@item
The id is spliced into the generated call through
@code{cmacs_dispatch_lisp_escape}, like the tool's name and arguments.
@item
The call still goes through @code{cmacs-brigade-call-tool}, so grants,
the takeover, confirmation, the audit hooks and the activity journal's
agent rule apply unchanged.  One consequence: a CLI agent's calls to
cmacs's tools now appear in its task's transaction log, where before
only an in-process agent's did.
@end itemize

@subheading What remains

@enumerate
@item
@strong{No scope, no task.}  In a build without the scoped-socket DEFUN,
or when the socket cannot be bound, provisioning falls back to the plain
socket behind the relay (as it always has).  Calls then carry no task
and @code{desktop_*} are refused.
@item
@strong{No real CLI run in the test suite.}  The tests drive the scoped
socket with a plain MCP client and read the configs back in every
dialect; they do not start @command{claude}, @command{opencode},
@command{codex} or @command{grok}.
@item
@strong{@code{desktop-podman}.}  The @code{desktop} server's command is
a host path to @command{gowl-mcp} and its socket a host path; whether
both exist inside the container depends on the image and mounts.  The
@code{desktop_*} brigade tools do not depend on either.
@item
A worker's environment cannot unset a variable, hence an empty
@env{DISPLAY}.
@end enumerate

@node Agent Desktop Tests
@section Tests

@command{make -C test check-cmacs} runs
@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-brigade-desktop-tests.el}.  All but one test run
against a stand-in for gowl, a unix socket server in the test's own
Emacs: the exact config text, both environments, the socket-path
refusal, grants, the takeover, coordinate mapping, the JSON-RPC client
(handshake, lines that answer nothing, @code{isError}, errors, a dead
socket, a timeout, ids answered as strings), the relay end to end, the
tools reaching only the caller's desktop, and the binary search passing
over a gowl with no MCP module.  For CLI agents: the provider's own
@code{:mcp-servers} provisioned through every registered config dialect,
a tool call made by a plain MCP client on a scoped socket resolving to
the scope's task (and to no other, whatever its arguments claim,
including a task id written to break out of its string), a scope for a
task with no desktop, a scope for no task, and the unscoped socket
refused.

@code{cmacs-brigade-desktop-test-real-start-and-stop} starts a real
headless gowl in a temporary directory, takes a picture, lists its
tools through the relay and stops it.  It runs only with
@env{CMACS_TEST_DESKTOP} set and a gowl present:

@example
CMACS_TEST_DESKTOP=1 src/emacs --batch -Q -L lisp/cmacs -L test/cmacs \
    -l ert -l test/cmacs/cmacs-brigade-desktop-tests.el \
    -f ert-run-tests-batch-and-exit
@end example

@node Agent Desktop Troubleshooting
@section Troubleshooting

@table @asis
@item @samp{no gowl could be started: ... has no mcp module}
Every gowl found was built without @code{MCP=1}.  Build one with it, or
install one; pin it with @code{cmacs-brigade-desktop-gowl-program}.
@item @samp{... does not run: ... libwlroots}
An old hand-built gowl ahead of the working one on @env{PATH}.  Remove
it, or pin the working one.
@item @samp{gowl did not open its sockets in 15 seconds}
No MCP module after all, or it died; read @file{server.log}.
@item @samp{... is 131 bytes, and a unix socket address holds 107}
The state directory is too deep.  Set
@code{cmacs-brigade-desktop-directory} to a shorter path.
@item @samp{this call came from no brigade task}
A desktop tool called from a chat, a harness buffer, or an MCP client
that is not a run's own agent.  Only a task's own agent has a desktop;
see @ref{Agent Desktop CLI Agents}.
@item The agent's clicks are refused
You hold the desktop.  @kbd{C-c C-r} in the viewer.
@item @samp{screen_text refused: ... tesseract}
No OCR program.  Install @code{tesseract} and
@code{tesseract-langpack-eng}.
@item A black picture
Nothing is open yet.  @code{desktop_launch} something, or @kbd{l} in
the viewer.
@item Replies that never seemed to arrive
gowl 0.5.0's MCP server answers id @code{1} as @code{"1"}.  Handled:
ids are compared as text.
@end table
